WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.630
OpenAI runs ChatGPT for eight hundred million users.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.260
The expectation is that they have built some custom-engineered distributed database to handle that load.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.890
They have not.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.520
They run on Postgres.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.150
The same Postgres you can install on your laptop in five minutes.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:23.780
The trick is not in the database, it is in how they shape the workload.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.285
Look at the constraint.

00:00:28.285 --> 00:00:32.789
Of those eight hundred million users, almost all of them are reading: scrolling chat history, opening conversations, listing past prompts.

00:00:32.789 --> 00:00:37.294
A small fraction are writing: sending a new message, starting a new chat.

00:00:37.294 --> 00:00:41.798
Reads outnumber writes by maybe a hundred to one.

00:00:41.798 --> 00:00:46.303
That asymmetry is the architectural opening.

00:00:46.303 --> 00:00:50.274
OpenAI's design: keep one writable Postgres primary, the single source of truth for every write.

00:00:50.274 --> 00:00:54.244
Then spin up fifty globally distributed read-only replicas.

00:00:54.244 --> 00:00:58.215
Every read request routes to a replica.

00:00:58.215 --> 00:01:02.186
Every write goes to the primary.

00:01:02.186 --> 00:01:06.156
The replicas catch up via streaming replication.

00:01:06.156 --> 00:01:10.127
One database engine, horizontally scaled in a way Postgres was designed for.

00:01:10.127 --> 00:01:13.718
Why not switch to a distributed database like Spanner or Cockroach?

00:01:13.718 --> 00:01:17.310
Two reasons.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.901
First, Postgres is mature.

00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:24.492
Decades of bug fixes, every edge case handled.

00:01:24.492 --> 00:01:28.084
Second, the workload doesn't need distributed writes.

00:01:28.084 --> 00:01:31.675
With one primary and replicas, you keep transaction guarantees and avoid the complexity of distributed consensus.

00:01:31.675 --> 00:01:36.806
The lesson is one many startups learn at scale.

00:01:36.806 --> 00:01:41.938
The hard problem in databases is not usually the database itself.

00:01:41.938 --> 00:01:47.070
It is figuring out which constraints your workload obeys, and picking the cheapest architecture that respects them.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:52.201
For a read-heavy workload, Postgres with read replicas takes you further than you would think.
